grazedbiomass#Definition#Methods.template:
The methodology focuses on measuring the biomass productivity of ecosystems suitable for livestock grazing in tonnes of biomass available for grazing by livestock. The methodology only measures the supply of this service by natural (including  semi-natural grassland) ecosystem types by masking global biomass production to these areas.  Supply from anthropogenic ecosystems (sown pastures) is associated with the conversion of natural ecosystems and loss of biodiversity. Hence, supply from anthropogenic ecosystems is not included. Additionally, we also aim to incorporate the estimated use of this service through incorporating spatial mapping of livestock forage demand. 

The approach is inspired by the Co$ting Nature forage for livestock production model @CITE(Mulligan2024), which has been used to map the service globally @cite(ChaplinKramer2023). By incorporating new, higher resolution spatial data @cite(Parente2025), livestock biomass intake requirements @cite(IPCC2019) and clearer definitions of grazed lands, we produce updated, annual layers.

@section(Potential grazed biomass supply)
Potential supply of the service is calculated based on the annual productivity of natural/semi-natural grasslands and other land cover classes (eventually transitioning to ecosystem types) identified as suitable for livestock grazing @cite(Parente2025). Annual uncalibrated gross primary productivity (gC/m2/year) (GPP) data is masked to natural lands suitable for livestock grazing @cite(Parente2024) @cite(Zhang2023) and converted to available biomass (tonnes dry matter/ha). A factor of 0.5 is used to convert GPP to net primary productivity (NPP), and light use efficiency factors (LUE) are applied based on the ecosystem present (grassland or shrubland), dry matter conversion factor = 2.7 and spatial scale factor = 0.01.

@section(Potential grazed biomass demand)
Total annual demand (D) is estimated based on the spatial distribution of livestock @cite(Leandro2025), by livestock type, and their mean estimated weights by continent/region (IPCC 2019 (Table 10A.5)). Estimated daily intake is assumed to be 2.5% of live body weight (kg/head/day) @cite(Arantes2018); @cite(Veloso2020). The livestock headcount per cell is multiplied by intake to estimate total daily demand, and by 365 and 0.001 to estimate the annual demand in tonnes/dry matter/year @cite(Veloso2020). 

@section(Potential grazed biomass use)
The final used service is calculated as the smallest value of the two calculations (the potential supply or the estimated use). If the intake requirement of the livestock is higher than the biomass production of the cell, it is assumed that the remainder is made up through additional supply of feed, or by managed seasonal migration of livestock (i.e., transhumance).
